select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-001","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What is the difference between commercial and non-commercial energy? Give an example of each.","back":"Commercial energy is traded at a market price (coal, oil, natural gas, grid electricity); non-commercial energy is not normally bought and sold (firewood, cattle dung, agricultural waste).","explain":"Commercial sources power industry, transport and urban households and are the basis of national energy statistics. Non-commercial sources are gathered free, mainly in rural areas, and are often left out of official consumption figures. Solar energy used for water heating is also treated as non-commercial in BEE questions.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 S7; BEE 2025 P1 Q44","order":1000},
{"id":"p1-002","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish primary and secondary energy sources.","back":"Primary energy is found or stored in nature (coal, crude oil, natural gas, biomass, solar, wind, hydro, nuclear fuel); secondary energy is produced by converting primary energy (electricity, steam, petrol/diesel from refining).","explain":"Primary sources are mostly converted in industrial utilities into secondary forms before end use. For example, coal is burnt in a power station to make electricity, and crude oil is refined into transport fuels. Every conversion step has losses, so saving one unit of secondary energy saves more than one unit of primary energy.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1001},
{"id":"p1-003","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish renewable and non-renewable energy, with an example of each.","back":"Renewable energy comes from sources that are naturally replenished and practically inexhaustible (solar, wind, hydro, biomass, geothermal); non-renewable energy comes from finite stocks that run out over time (coal, oil, natural gas, nuclear fuel).","explain":"Renewables are generally low-carbon and do not deplete. Fossil fuels took millions of years to form and are being used up much faster than they form. Coal is the standard 'non-renewable' answer in BEE objective questions.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q43, S7","order":1002},
{"id":"p1-004","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"How is the Reserves-to-Production (R/P) ratio calculated, and what does it tell you?","back":"R/P = reserves remaining at the end of the year ÷ production in that year. It gives the number of years the remaining reserves would last if production continued at that rate.","explain":"India's coal R/P ratio is much higher than its oil and gas R/P ratios. That is why coal is seen as the fuel that can give secure domestic supply for decades, while oil and gas depend heavily on imports. The ratio changes as new reserves are found or production rises.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q34; BEE 2025 P1 Q34","order":1003},
{"id":"p1-005","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Define energy intensity.","back":"Energy intensity = Energy consumption ÷ GDP (for example, toe per million US$ of GDP). It shows how much energy the economy uses to produce one unit of economic output.","explain":"High energy intensity means energy is being turned into GDP inefficiently, or that the economy relies on energy-heavy industry. Low energy intensity usually means energy is being turned into GDP efficiently. It is a macro indicator used to compare countries over time.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q23; BEE 2025 P1 S2","order":1004},
{"id":"p1-006","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Why does a lower energy intensity not necessarily mean higher energy efficiency?","back":"Energy intensity also depends on economic structure (share of services against heavy industry), climate, geography, lifestyle, exchange rates and how much non-commercial energy is counted. It is not only technical efficiency.","explain":"A service-led economy can have low energy intensity even with inefficient equipment. A country with a large steel or cement base can show high intensity even with efficient plants. Energy intensity is therefore only a rough proxy for efficiency.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 S6","order":1005},
{"id":"p1-007","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Why is GDP converted using Purchasing Power Parity (PPP) when comparing energy intensity across countries?","back":"PPP values every country's GDP at a uniform price level. The comparison then reflects differences in real economic volume, not market exchange-rate distortions.","explain":"Market exchange rates undervalue the output of developing countries, where many goods and services are cheaper. GDP at market exchange rates therefore makes their energy intensity look much higher than it really is. PPP-based GDP gives a fairer comparison.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 S6; BEE 2025 P1 Q35","order":1006},
{"id":"p1-008","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"How are energy conservation and energy efficiency related?","back":"Energy efficiency means using less energy for the same output or service (lower energy per unit). Energy conservation means reducing overall energy consumption, which can include behaviour change or doing without.","explain":"Efficiency is about technology and how well equipment performs, such as an efficient motor doing the same work with less input. Conservation is broader: it includes efficiency and also avoiding waste, such as switching off idle equipment. Both cut demand, and efficiency does not require reducing comfort or output.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q36","order":1007},
{"id":"p1-009","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Why is energy saved through Demand Side Management (DSM) treated like new supply-side capacity?","back":"Each MW of demand avoided by DSM does the same job as adding a MW of generation (a 'negawatt'), and it costs much less than building new power plants.","explain":"DSM programmes run by utilities or DISCOMs shift or reduce load, especially at peak, through efficient appliances, time-of-day tariffs and load management. The aims are to lower peak demand, defer capital spending on capacity, and improve the load factor. Increasing generator load to meet the peak is NOT a DSM objective.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q1; BEE 2024 P1 Q10","order":1008},
{"id":"p1-010","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What was the main aim of India's Accelerated Power Development and Reform Programme (APDRP)?","back":"To reduce Aggregate Technical & Commercial (AT&C) losses in distribution through energy accounting and audits, metering, and strengthening of the sub-transmission and distribution network.","explain":"AT&C losses combine technical losses (I²R in lines and transformers) with commercial losses (theft, unmetered supply, billing and collection gaps). APDRP and its restructured successor (R-APDRP) funded IT-based energy accounting and network upgrades for state utilities.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q2","order":1009},
{"id":"p1-011","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Why was Availability Based Tariff (ABT) introduced in India?","back":"To improve grid discipline and frequency control. It does this with a tariff of three parts: a capacity (fixed) charge, an energy (variable) charge, and an Unscheduled Interchange (UI) charge linked to frequency.","explain":"Under ABT, a generator is paid its capacity charge for the availability it declares, and its energy charge for energy actually scheduled. Deviations from schedule are priced by grid frequency: over-drawing at low frequency is expensive. This rewards keeping to schedule and supporting frequency.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q32","order":1010},
{"id":"p1-012","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"problem","front":"Country X consumes 500 Mtoe of energy and has a GDP of US$ 2.5 trillion. Country Y consumes 300 Mtoe and has a GDP of US$ 1.2 trillion. Calculate the energy intensity of each in toe per million US$ and state which uses energy more productively.","back":"X: 200 toe per million US$; Y: 250 toe per million US$. Country X has the lower energy intensity.","explain":"Energy intensity = energy ÷ GDP, so a lower value means more GDP per unit of energy. Before concluding that X is more energy efficient, remember that economic structure, climate and PPP effects also affect intensity.","steps":[{"prompt":"Express GDP in million US$.","answer":"X: 2.5 trillion = 2,500,000 million US$; Y: 1.2 trillion = 1,200,000 million US$.","explain":"1 trillion = 10⁶ million."},{"prompt":"Express energy in toe.","answer":"X: 500 × 10⁶ toe; Y: 300 × 10⁶ toe.","explain":"1 Mtoe = 10⁶ toe."},{"prompt":"Compute energy intensity = energy ÷ GDP.","answer":"X: 500×10⁶ ÷ 2.5×10⁶ = 200 toe/million US$; Y: 300×10⁶ ÷ 1.2×10⁶ = 250 toe/million US$.","explain":"EI = Energy consumption ÷ GDP."},{"prompt":"Interpret the result.","answer":"X has the lower EI, so it produces more GDP per toe.","explain":"Lower intensity suggests better productivity of energy use, but is not by itself proof of technical efficiency."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 S2 (modified)","order":1011},
{"id":"p1-b01-001","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"By roughly what percentage does global energy demand grow each year, and what share of primary energy comes from fossil fuels?","back":"About 2.4% per year; over 85% of primary energy comes from fossil fuels.","explain":"The guide book opens the chapter with these two numbers to show why the present pattern of use, especially of oil, cannot last. Demand keeps rising while the resource base is finite, so efficiency and conservation become a necessity rather than an option.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p19","order":1012},
{"id":"p1-b01-002","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Arrange the forms of coal in order of increasing rank (age/carbon content), starting from peat.","back":"Peat → brown coal (lignite) → sub-bituminous → bituminous → anthracite.","explain":"Coal forms as plant matter is buried and transformed by heat and pressure over time (Figure 1.3). Lignite is the youngest and lowest-grade true coal; anthracite has the highest carbon content. The BP tables group reserves as 'anthracite and bituminous' and 'sub-bituminous and lignite'.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p21","order":1013},
{"id":"p1-b01-003","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What were the world's proven coal reserves at the end of 2013, and how long would they last at the then production rate?","back":"About 892 billion tonnes (891,531 million t), enough for about 113 years (BP Statistical Review 2014).","explain":"Coal is the most abundant and most widely spread fossil fuel, with recoverable reserves in around 75 countries. The 113-year figure is the world R/P ratio at end-2013 production. It is a common objective question: 'coal on our planet is expected to last about 113 years'.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p21","order":1014},
{"id":"p1-b01-004","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"True or false: once established, a fuel's Reserves-to-Production (R/P) ratio stays constant.","back":"False. The R/P ratio changes every year because both reserves (new finds, revisions) and production change.","explain":"R/P = reserves remaining at the end of a year ÷ production in that year. It tells you how long reserves would last if production stayed at that year's level. New discoveries raise the numerator and rising output raises the denominator, so the ratio is recomputed every year.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p22","order":1015},
{"id":"p1-b01-005","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Which five countries held the largest proven coal reserves at the end of 2013 (in order)?","back":"USA (26.6%), Russia (17.6%), China (12.8%), Australia (8.6%), India (6.8%).","explain":"From Table 1.1 (BP 2014): USA ~237 billion t, Russia ~157, China ~114.5, Australia ~76.4, India ~60.6 billion t. China's R/P was only about 31 years because its output is enormous, while India's was about 100 years.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p22","order":1016},
{"id":"p1-b01-006","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Which country was the world's largest coal producer in 2013, and roughly what share did it have?","back":"China, about 3,680 million tonnes, roughly 47% of world output (2013).","explain":"World coal production in 2013 was about 7,896 million t. The US came second with about 893 Mt. India produced about 605 Mt. The book notes China alone now uses as much coal as the rest of the world, and most coal demand comes from the power sector.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p23","order":1017},
{"id":"p1-b01-007","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What were the world's proven crude oil reserves at the end of 2013, and roughly what share lay in the Middle East?","back":"About 1,687.9 billion barrels, with almost 48% in the Middle East (BP 2014).","explain":"Table 1.3 lists Venezuela (298.3 bn bbl, 17.7%) and Saudi Arabia (265.9 bn bbl, 15.8%) at the top, followed by Canada, Iran and Iraq. India had only about 5.7 billion barrels (0.3%). The world R/P for oil at end-2013 was about 53 years.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p23","order":1018},
{"id":"p1-b01-008","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"About how many litres are in one barrel of oil?","back":"About 160 litres (1 barrel ≈ 159 L, rounded to 160 L in the guide book).","explain":"Oil reserves and production are quoted in barrels internationally and in tonnes in Indian statistics. Remembering the conversion helps when you move between the two, together with the oil's density.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p23","order":1019},
{"id":"p1-b01-009","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Which country was the largest oil producer in 2013, and what is notable about the United States' position?","back":"Saudi Arabia (≈542 Mt/yr, 13.1%), closely followed by Russia and the US. The US ranked third in production but only tenth in proven reserves.","explain":"World oil output in 2013 was about 4,130 Mt. India produced about 42 Mt, about 1% of the total. A big producer with small reserves depletes faster, which is why the book warns that many non-Middle-East producers could largely deplete their fields and the world may come to depend on the Middle East.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p24","order":1020},
{"id":"p1-b01-010","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What is the average R/P ratio of Middle East conventional oil, and how much would global oil reserves grow if unconventional oil is counted?","back":"About 78 years for the Middle East; counting unconventional oil would make global reserves about four times larger than current conventional reserves.","explain":"Unconventional resources include oil shale, oil sands, extra heavy oil and natural bitumen. The book expects oil's main use to shift towards transport and petrochemicals, and its top position to be challenged by natural gas.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p24","order":1021},
{"id":"p1-b01-011","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish oil shale and oil sands (tar sands).","back":"Oil shale is sedimentary rock containing solid bituminous material (kerogen) that releases petroleum-like liquid when heated (pyrolysis). Oil sands are a mix of clay, sand, water and bitumen, mined and processed to extract the bitumen, which is refined into oil.","explain":"Both are 'unconventional' oil. The difference is that oil shale holds kerogen that must be chemically converted by heating, while oil sands already contain heavy viscous bitumen that is separated and then upgraded.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p25","order":1022},
{"id":"p1-b01-012","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What were the world's proven natural gas reserves at the end of 2013, how many years would they last, and which three countries held the most?","back":"About 186 trillion m³ (185.7), roughly 55 years of production; Iran (18.2%), Russia (16.8%) and Qatar (13.3%). India had about 0.7%.","explain":"Natural gas is mainly methane with small amounts of ethane, propane, butane and pentane, and it is processed to remove almost everything except methane before use. It ranks third in use after oil and coal. Like oil, its reserves are concentrated in a few countries and fields.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p25","order":1023},
{"id":"p1-b01-013","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What was global primary energy consumption in 2013, and what were the shares of oil, coal and natural gas?","back":"About 12,730 Mtoe; oil 33%, coal 30%, natural gas 24% (hydro 7%, nuclear 4%, renewables 2%).","explain":"From Table 1.6 (BP 2014). Note the text says 'end of 2014' for the same 12,730 Mtoe figure but the source is BP June 2014, so it is really 2013 data. Gas demand is expected to grow as industrial countries act to cut CO₂ emissions.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p26","order":1024},
{"id":"p1-b01-014","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Which country was the largest energy consumer in 2013, and how did India's consumption compare?","back":"China (about 2,852 Mtoe, 22.4% of world). India's 595 Mtoe was about 4.7% of the world, 26% of the USA's and 21% of China's consumption (2013).","explain":"The USA was second at about 2,266 Mtoe (17.8%). A common exam trap is to answer 'USA'. India's absolute consumption is small relative to its population, as the per-capita figures in the chapter also show.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p26","order":1025},
{"id":"p1-b01-015","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"How is 'final energy consumption' defined, and what is excluded from it?","back":"It is the energy available to end users after conversion from primary energy: the sum of consumption in the end-use sectors. Energy used for transformation and for own use by energy-producing industries is excluded.","explain":"Final consumption therefore reflects energy actually delivered to consumers. Globally, industry uses almost 50% of final energy, then transport (20%), residential (18%) and commercial (12%) (US EIA figures quoted in the book).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p26","order":1026},
{"id":"p1-b01-016","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What was India's primary commercial energy consumption mix in 2013?","back":"Total 595 Mtoe: coal 54.5%, oil 29.5%, natural gas 7.8%, hydro 5.0%, renewables 2.0%, nuclear 1.3% (Table 1.8).","explain":"Coal dominates both India's production and consumption. Oil is the second-largest source and is mostly imported. Gas's share in India (7.8%) is far below the world average of about 24%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p27","order":1027},
{"id":"p1-b01-017","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"In India, roughly what shares of total primary energy consumption come from commercial and non-commercial energy?","back":"Commercial about 74%; non-commercial about 26%.","explain":"Non-commercial energy (firewood, cattle dung, agricultural waste) is mostly used in rural households and is often left out of energy statistics, so it is easy to underestimate. The figure shows how much India still relies on traditional fuels.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p27","order":1028},
{"id":"p1-b01-018","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What are India's coal reserves, and how long might they last at the current R/P ratio?","back":"About 60.6 billion t (hard coal 56.1 bn t, soft coal 4.5 bn t), about 6.8% of world reserves, enough for about 100 years. Jharkhand and Odisha hold almost 51% (as of 31 March 2013).","explain":"Indian reserves are concentrated in the eastern and south-central parts of the country. Hard coal means anthracite and bituminous; soft coal means sub-bituminous and lignite.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p27","order":1029},
{"id":"p1-b01-019","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"State three characteristics of Indian coal mining and coal quality mentioned in the guide book.","back":"Over 80% is mined by open-cast mining (up to 150–300 m depth). The coal has high ash (30–45%) and a calorific value of about 3,000–4,500 kcal/kg. The power sector uses about 75% of the coal produced.","explain":"High-ash coal makes efficient combustion and fly-ash management hard for power stations. That is why higher-CV, low-ash coal is imported, mainly from Indonesia, Australia and South Africa, and coking coal is imported by the steel sector.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p27","order":1030},
{"id":"p1-b01-020","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What is the Clean Energy Cess on coal, since when has it applied, and at what rate (as stated in the book)?","back":"A tax of Rs 100 per tonne on all coal, peat and lignite mined in India or imported, levied since 1 July 2010.","explain":"The cess raises funds for research, development and deployment of cleaner and renewable energy technologies. Exam MCQs ask which fuel carries it: the answer is coal, not electricity or diesel. The rate is as given in the 2015 guide book.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p28","order":1031},
{"id":"p1-b01-021","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"How much coal did India import in 2012-13, what share of requirement was this, and who were the main suppliers?","back":"About 145.8 million t (≈20% of annual requirement) in 2012-13, mainly from Indonesia, then Australia and South Africa.","explain":"Imports rose sharply after 2011 (68.9 Mt in 2010-11, 102.9 Mt in 2011-12). Steel makers import coking coal for quality reasons. Coastal power stations and cement plants import non-coking coal for logistics and cost.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p28","order":1032},
{"id":"p1-b01-022","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What carbon-intensity commitment had India made, according to the guide book?","back":"To reduce the carbon intensity of its economy by 20–25% from 2005 levels by 2020 (a domestic commitment).","explain":"The book notes that relying on coal for large-scale rural electrification could make it harder to meet this target. Carbon intensity is emissions per unit of GDP, not total emissions.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p29","order":1033},
{"id":"p1-b01-023","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Summarise India's oil position as given in the guide book (reserves, share, main fields, R/P, import dependence).","back":"Reserves about 5.7 bn barrels (800 Mt), only ~0.3% of world reserves. Main fields are Bombay High, upper Assam, Cambay and the Krishna-Godavari basin. R/P is about 17.5 years, and India imports over 75% of its crude.","explain":"Production was about 42 Mt against consumption of about 175 Mt (2013). India was the fourth-largest oil consumer. Transport is the biggest user of petroleum products, followed by domestic and industry. Crude imports in 2013-14 were 189.2 Mt costing about Rs 8.65 lakh crore.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p29","order":1034},
{"id":"p1-b01-024","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"To what temperature is natural gas cooled to make LNG, and by how much does liquefaction shrink its volume?","back":"About −161 °C; liquefaction cuts the volume about 600 times.","explain":"LNG is shipped in special cryogenic tankers and regasified at receiving terminals. By contrast, CNG is natural gas compressed to under 1% of its atmospheric volume and stored in cylinders at about 200–248 kg/cm². It can run converted petrol (bi-fuel) vehicles.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p29","order":1035},
{"id":"p1-b01-025","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Summarise India's natural gas position: reserves, R/P, share of consumption, and main consuming sectors.","back":"Reserves about 1.4 trillion m³ (0.7% of world); R/P about 40 years; gas is about 7.8% of India's fuel consumption versus a world average of about 24% (2013). Power generation and fertiliser use about 62% of gas.","explain":"About 66% of Indian gas comes from offshore fields; onshore output is mainly from Assam, Gujarat and Andhra Pradesh. Consumption (51.4 bcm) exceeded production (33.7 bcm) in 2013, so LNG is imported. The drawbacks the book lists are price unpredictability and uncertain availability.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p30","order":1036},
{"id":"p1-b01-026","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What was India's installed power capacity in February 2014, and how was it split?","back":"About 2,38,743 MW: thermal 1,63,305 MW (68.4%; coal 58.9%, gas 9.0%), hydro 40,195 MW (16.8%), renewables 29,463 MW (12.3%), nuclear 5,780 MW (2.4%).","explain":"Thermal power is the major source of electricity generation in India. Gross generation in 2013-14 was about 881,786 million kWh, and the country faced an energy shortage of 3.8% and a peak shortage of 3.3% (Ministry of Power).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p31","order":1037},
{"id":"p1-b01-027","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Why is India's ability to develop nuclear power restricted?","back":"Indian uranium ore contains only about 0.1% uranium, compared with 12–13% in ores mined abroad. Domestic uranium can support only about 10,000 MW of nuclear capacity.","explain":"In 2015 India ran 21 nuclear units at seven locations totalling 5,780 MW (BWRs and PHWRs), about 2% of installed capacity. The Department of Atomic Energy planned 63,000 MW by 2032. The MCQ answer to 'nuclear development is constrained by…' is 'all of the above': low ore grade, inadequate supply and import constraints.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p31","order":1038},
{"id":"p1-b01-028","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What is India's economically viable hydro potential, and where is most of it?","back":"Over 84,000 MW at 60% load factor, about 80% of it in the Brahmaputra, Indus and Ganges basins, plus about 15,000 MW in small hydro.","explain":"Hydro's share of generated units had fallen to about 17% by 2013. To speed up development, projects up to 25 MW were placed under the Ministry of New and Renewable Energy (MNRE), while larger projects stay with the Ministry of Power.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p31","order":1039},
{"id":"p1-b01-029","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Which sector consumes the largest share of India's commercial energy, and which comes next?","back":"Industry, about 44%, followed by transport (about 17%) (Figure 1.4, TERI).","explain":"Specific energy consumption in major Indian industries is well above global benchmarks. For example, iron and steel uses 25.5–34.2 GJ/t in India against 16.5–18.5 GJ/t worldwide, and aluminium 75.6–83.2 versus 70.5–73.0 GJ/t (Table 1.13). That gap is the efficiency opportunity.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p32","order":1040},
{"id":"p1-b01-030","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What growth rate and share of petroleum products does the guide book give for India's transport sector, and what is the 2030 projection?","back":"It grows about 16% a year and uses around 40% of petroleum products. By 2030, road vehicles are projected to take 86% of transport energy, aviation 9%, and rail, marine and others 5%.","explain":"This is why mass transport, fuel-efficiency standards and moving freight to railways appear in the energy-security and integrated-energy-policy measures.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p33","order":1041},
{"id":"p1-b01-031","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"How do rural and urban households meet their cooking needs, and how is domestic electricity used?","back":"Rural households meet about 85% of cooking needs from biomass; urban households meet about 56% from LPG. About 70% of domestic electricity is used for lighting, and the remaining 30% for refrigeration, air-conditioning and gadgets.","explain":"About 70% of India's population is rural but accounts for only about 42% of demand for oil, gas and electricity. The high lighting share explains why lamp programmes such as BLY and LED replacement were early priorities.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p33","order":1042},
{"id":"p1-b01-032","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What was India's per-capita electricity consumption in 2012-13, and how did it compare with the world average?","back":"About 917 kWh per person per year, against a world average of about 2,430 kWh.","explain":"It rose from 819 kWh (2010-11) to 884 kWh (2011-12) and 917 kWh (2012-13) (CEA). Comparable figures quoted are the USA 13,246, Canada 16,473, Japan 7,848, UK 6,206 and China 3,298 kWh.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p34","order":1043},
{"id":"p1-b01-033","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"To sustain 8% GDP growth up to 2031-32, how much must India's energy supply grow?","back":"Primary energy supply 3–4 times, electricity installed capacity 6–7 times, and annual coal requirement nearly 3 times (to over 2 billion t/yr).","explain":"These targets come from the Integrated Energy Policy. Oil import dependence was expected to rise from about 75% to over 90%, and the share of natural gas was expected to reach 20–25% by 2030-32.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p34","order":1044}
]$cards$::jsonb);
